1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,087
That's the pattern worth naming, and there are really only four moves available:

2
00:00:05,087 --> 00:00:10,061
prompt it better, retrieve the right information, let it take actions, or change the

3
00:00:10,061 --> 00:00:10,830
model itself.

4
00:00:10,830 --> 00:00:14,975
Three questions, asked in order, tell you which one you actually need.

5
00:00:14,975 --> 00:00:17,817
Get the order right and you'll notice something.

6
00:00:17,817 --> 00:00:22,673
The expensive option almost never turns out to be the first one you needed to try.

7
00:00:22,673 --> 00:00:27,469
It usually is the last box on this tree, reached only once the cheaper three have

8
00:00:27,469 --> 00:00:31,910
genuinely been ruled out, not skipped past because it sounded more serious.

9
00:00:31,910 --> 00:00:36,528
That matters because these four options don't cost the same to be wrong about.

10
00:00:36,528 --> 00:00:39,489
Ship the wrong prompt and you fix it before lunch.

11
00:00:39,489 --> 00:00:44,344
Commit to a fine-tune on the wrong assumption and you've spent days of engineering

12
00:00:44,344 --> 00:00:46,476
time and a training run finding out.

13
00:00:46,476 --> 00:00:51,390
The tree exists to make sure that cost difference actually shapes the order you try

14
00:00:51,390 --> 00:00:55,950
things in, instead of whichever option sounds most impressive in the planning

15
00:00:55,950 --> 00:00:56,423
meeting.

16
00:00:56,973 --> 00:01:00,145
Walk it in order, because the order is the whole point.

17
00:01:00,145 --> 00:01:04,701
First question: does this need facts the model wasn't trained on, or facts that

18
00:01:04,701 --> 00:01:08,565
change, like this week's prices or this customer's account history?

19
00:01:08,565 --> 00:01:10,468
If yes, stop, you need retrieval.

20
00:01:10,468 --> 00:01:15,255
That's RAG, and it answers more "the model doesn't know enough" complaints than any

21
00:01:15,255 --> 00:01:16,582
other box on this tree.

22
00:01:16,582 --> 00:01:19,292
If the facts are fine, ask the second question.

23
00:01:19,292 --> 00:01:23,848
Does this need multiple steps, or calling some other system: booking something,

24
00:01:23,848 --> 00:01:27,366
updating a record, checking a second source before answering?

25
00:01:27,366 --> 00:01:32,095
If yes, you need an agent, orchestration that can decide what to do next, not just

26
00:01:32,095 --> 00:01:33,076
what to say next.

27
00:01:33,076 --> 00:01:37,805
Only if both are no do you reach the third question: has prompting actually failed

28
00:01:37,805 --> 00:01:39,765
real testing, not "might it fail"?

29
00:01:39,765 --> 00:01:44,091
If a better prompt genuinely can't hold the format or behaviour you need, a

30
00:01:44,091 --> 00:01:45,533
fine-tune earns its cost.

31
00:01:45,533 --> 00:01:49,743
If prompting hasn't been seriously tried yet, the answer is still prompt.

32
00:01:49,743 --> 00:01:54,529
One more distinction: "multiple steps" means deciding what to do next based on what

33
00:01:54,529 --> 00:01:57,759
just happened, not multiple steps inside one model call.

34
00:01:57,759 --> 00:02:02,373
Look something up, decide whether to escalate, then act: that's the branching an

35
00:02:02,373 --> 00:02:06,525
agent buys you, and why it's harder to evaluate than anything else here.

36
00:02:06,525 --> 00:02:08,428
Section six covers that properly.

37
00:02:08,978 --> 00:02:13,588
Here's the same four options, mapped onto the stack from last lecture, and the

38
00:02:13,588 --> 00:02:15,361
pattern is worth sitting with.

39
00:02:15,361 --> 00:02:20,208
A prompt change and an agent both live in orchestration, the layer you can iterate

40
00:02:20,208 --> 00:02:20,858
on fastest.

41
00:02:20,858 --> 00:02:25,645
RAG lives in your data layer, still cheap to change: swap what gets retrieved and

42
00:02:25,645 --> 00:02:26,945
test again in minutes.

43
00:02:26,945 --> 00:02:31,614
A fine-tune is the only one of the four that touches the foundation model layer

44
00:02:31,614 --> 00:02:36,343
itself, and that's exactly why it's the slowest and most expensive to get wrong.

45
00:02:36,343 --> 00:02:38,529
Read the iteration cost column again.

46
00:02:38,529 --> 00:02:41,780
It climbs by roughly an order of magnitude at each row.

47
00:02:41,780 --> 00:02:45,799
That column alone is the argument for asking the questions in order.

48
00:02:45,799 --> 00:02:50,764
You want to exhaust the cheap, fast options before committing to the one measured in

49
00:02:50,764 --> 00:02:51,709
days, not hours.

50
00:02:51,709 --> 00:02:55,019
There's a second reason the layer matters, beyond speed.

51
00:02:55,019 --> 00:02:58,211
Whoever owns that layer is who ends up doing the work.

52
00:02:58,211 --> 00:03:02,584
A prompt change is often something you can propose in a document yourself.

53
00:03:02,584 --> 00:03:05,717
RAG usually needs someone who owns the data pipeline.

54
00:03:05,717 --> 00:03:10,622
A fine-tune needs an engineer who can run a training job, evaluate it properly, and

55
00:03:10,622 --> 00:03:12,573
decide whether it's safe to ship.

56
00:03:12,573 --> 00:03:17,360
Naming the layer tells you not just how long the fix takes, but whose calendar it

57
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:18,897
actually needs to land on.

58
00:03:19,447 --> 00:03:24,113
Here's the tree, run for real, on a complaint you'll actually hear: answers are

59
00:03:24,113 --> 00:03:25,708
citing last month's prices.

60
00:03:25,708 --> 00:03:30,492
The instinct in the room is often "let's fine-tune it on the current sheet." Walk

61
00:03:30,492 --> 00:03:33,859
that against question one and it falls apart immediately.

62
00:03:33,859 --> 00:03:35,158
Prices change monthly.

63
00:03:35,158 --> 00:03:40,001
A fine-tune baked in today is wrong again in four weeks, and each correction means

64
00:03:40,001 --> 00:03:42,659
retraining and re-evaluating before it ships.

65
00:03:42,659 --> 00:03:46,085
The actual fix was sitting at question one the whole time.

66
00:03:46,085 --> 00:03:50,751
Retrieve the current price sheet at answer time, and the fix updates itself the

67
00:03:50,751 --> 00:03:53,173
moment someone edits the source document.

68
00:03:53,173 --> 00:03:57,957
No retraining, no waiting on a batch job, no drift between what the model "knows"

69
00:03:57,957 --> 00:04:00,025
and what's actually true this week.

70
00:04:00,025 --> 00:04:02,919
Same complaint, same team, same model underneath.

71
00:04:02,919 --> 00:04:06,758
The only thing that changed was which layer they looked at first.

72
00:04:07,308 --> 00:04:11,565
Say the honest caveat, because this tree can look like it's asking you to pick

73
00:04:11,565 --> 00:04:14,839
exactly one box, and that's rarely how a real feature works.

74
00:04:14,839 --> 00:04:19,260
A support agent that looks up your order and then cancels it is doing RAG to find

75
00:04:19,260 --> 00:04:21,879
the order and acting to cancel it, both at once.

76
00:04:21,879 --> 00:04:24,717
The tree isn't asking you to choose a single answer.

77
00:04:24,717 --> 00:04:28,810
It's asking you to name every box that applies, in order, so you don't skip

78
00:04:28,810 --> 00:04:33,121
retrieval on your way to building an agent that didn't actually need to be one.

79
00:04:33,671 --> 00:04:38,407
That's the entire tool, and notice where it puts the burden of proof: on the

80
00:04:38,407 --> 00:04:40,650
expensive option, not the cheap one.

81
00:04:40,650 --> 00:04:43,828
Nobody has to justify trying a better prompt first.

82
00:04:43,828 --> 00:04:48,875
The justification is owed by whoever wants to skip straight to the box that takes

83
00:04:48,875 --> 00:04:51,119
days and needs its own training run.

84
00:04:51,119 --> 00:04:52,676
The exercise is specific.

85
00:04:52,676 --> 00:04:57,911
Find a real proposal, yours or someone else's, that reached for a fine-tune, and run

86
00:04:57,911 --> 00:04:59,718
it backward through the tree.

87
00:04:59,718 --> 00:05:04,765
More often than you'd expect, it stops at question one or two, and the actual fix

88
00:05:04,765 --> 00:05:07,756
was retrieval or a better prompt the whole time.

89
00:05:08,306 --> 00:05:09,983
One thing before you move on.

90
00:05:09,983 --> 00:05:14,726
Run the three questions, in order, against whatever AI feature is actually on your

91
00:05:14,726 --> 00:05:15,651
plate right now.

92
00:05:15,651 --> 00:05:20,336
Stop at the first yes, then check whether it's really one box: most real features

93
00:05:20,336 --> 00:05:25,136
are two of the four working together, and naming both is more useful than forcing a

94
00:05:25,136 --> 00:05:25,946
single answer.

95
00:05:25,946 --> 00:05:30,689
Next lecture takes the RAG box from this tree and opens it up: what embeddings and

96
00:05:30,689 --> 00:05:33,927
vector search actually do, without the underlying maths.
